{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>Please, this people has sinned.</b> From here the High Priest on Yom HaKippurim would use the expression אנא, and from Moses he would take the linguistic formula to say אנא ה' חטאתי. And so they expounded in tractate Yoma: atonement was said at Horev (Exodus 32) — אולי אכפרה — and atonement was said on Yom HaKippurim; just as there it is with אנא, so here with אנא. And from where do we know it is with the Name? It is derived by the verbal link כפרה–כפרה from the eglah arufah: just as there it is with the Name, as it is written (Deuteronomy 21) ״כפר לעמך ישראל אשר פדית ה׳״, so here with the Name." "<b>And know</b> that the way of those who pray with אנא is to invoke the Name, as in (Psalms 118) ״אנא ה' הושיעה נא״, and likewise the High Priest on Yom HaKippurim: אנא השם, and likewise Samson (Judges 16) ״אדני ה' זכרני נא וחזקני נא אך הפעם״. And the reason Moses did not mention it thus — it is possible to say that he was ashamed to invoke the special Name in which the Torah was given, since Israel sinned against it and transgressed against the Torah. And even though he did not mention it, it is alluded to in the word אנא, which is language of supplication and embraces both Names that are among the thirteen attributes." "<b>Or</b> it is further possible to say: because he needed to spell out the sin and mention ״ויעשו להם אלהי זהב״, therefore he did not wish to invoke the Name — for there is no unification in a place of separation; in the manner of what they expounded: what has the King to do in the house of slaughtering, what has the priest to do in the cemetery? And so we find that once they made a god of gold, the Shekhinah withdrew from them and did not dwell among them except outside the camp, as it is said (Exodus 33:7) ״והיה כל מבקש ה' יצא אל אהל מועד אשר מחוץ למחנה״."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" 